{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-code:INFNet --start-line 190 --lines 145'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:INFNet\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/INFNet.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"e3750a73af1ceb6b2be4bf9355b70a9ad356985a3c465ef27238ef7e0f28fb4c\",\n  \"total_lines\": 313,\n  \"start_line\": 190,\n  \"next_line\": 314,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"190: \\n191:     def __init__(self, num_layers, token_dim, num_c_hubs, num_s_hubs, num_t_hubs, expansion_factor=4):\\n192:         super(INFNetBlocks, self).__init__()\\n193:         self.num_layers = num_layers\\n194: \\n195:         # Use nn.ModuleList to stack multiple layers of Aggregation and Broadcast respectively\\n196:         self.aggregation_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n197:             Aggregation(token_dim) for _ in range(num_layers)\\n198:         ])\\n199:         self.broadcast_layers = nn.ModuleList([\\n200:             Broadcast(token_dim, num_c_hubs, num_s_hubs, num_t_hubs, expansion_factor)\\n201:             for _ in range(num_layers)\\n202:         ])\\n203: \\n204:     def forward(self, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_shared, s_mask=None):\\n205:         # Equation (4): Task Hubs are composed of Specific and Shared\\n206:         t_hubs = torch.cat([t_orgs, t_shared], dim=1)\\n207:         # Iterative update layer by layer\\n208:         for i in range(self.num_layers):\\n209:             # Phase 1: Multi-View Global Aggregation\\n210:             c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs = self.aggregation_layers[i](\\n211:                 c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, s_mask\\n212:             )\\n213: \\n214:             # Phase 2: Global-to-Local Affine Broadcast\\n215:             c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs = self.broadcast_layers[i](\\n216:                 c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs\\n217:             )\\n218: \\n219:         return t_orgs\\n220: \\n221: \\n222: class Aggregation(nn.Module):\\n223:     \\\"\\\"\\\" Multi-View Global Aggregation\\n224:     Keep SDPA mask-free to preserve FlashAttention eligibility, accepting minor attention leakage.\\n225:     \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n226:     def __init__(self, token_dim):\\n227:         super(Aggregation, self).__init__()\\n228:         # Shared Keys/Values projection to reduce calculations\\n229:         self.k_proj_c = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n230:         self.v_proj_c = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n231:         self.k_proj_s = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n232:         self.v_proj_s = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n233:         self.k_proj_t = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n234:         self.v_proj_t = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n235: \\n236:         # Independent Hub aggregator for each group\\n237:         self.c_agg = HubSpecificAggregator(token_dim)\\n238:         self.s_agg = HubSpecificAggregator(token_dim)\\n239:         self.t_agg = HubSpecificAggregator(token_dim)\\n240: \\n241:     def forward(self, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, s_mask=None):\\n242:         K_c, V_c = self.k_proj_c(c_orgs), self.v_proj_c(c_orgs)\\n243:         K_s, V_s = self.k_proj_s(s_orgs), self.v_proj_s(s_orgs)\\n244:         K_t, V_t = self.k_proj_t(t_orgs), self.v_proj_t(t_orgs)\\n245: \\n246:         # Processing sequence Mask broadcast dimensions (B, 1, T)\\n247:         if s_mask is not None:\\n248:             K_s = K_s * s_mask.unsqueeze(-1).float()\\n249:             V_s = V_s * s_mask.unsqueeze(-1).float()\\n250: \\n251:         c_hubs_out = self.c_agg(c_hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t)\\n252:         s_hubs_out = self.s_agg(s_hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t)\\n253:         t_hubs_out = self.t_agg(t_hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t)\\n254: \\n255:         return c_hubs_out, s_hubs_out, t_hubs_out\\n256: \\n257: class HubSpecificAggregator(nn.Module):\\n258:     # Keep SDPA mask-free to preserve FlashAttention eligibility, accepting minor attention leakage.\\n259:     def __init__(self, token_dim):\\n260:         super(HubSpecificAggregator, self).__init__()\\n261:         self.q_proj = nn.Linear(token_dim, token_dim)\\n262:         self.fuse = nn.Linear(token_dim * 3, token_dim)\\n263:         self.norm = nn.LayerNorm(token_dim)\\n264:         self.dot_attention = ScaledDotProductAttention()\\n265: \\n266:     def forward(self, hubs, K_c, V_c, K_s, V_s, K_t, V_t):\\n267:         Q = self.q_proj(hubs)\\n268:         scale = Q.size(-1) ** 0.5\\n269:         Z_c, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K_c, V_c, scale=scale)\\n270:         Z_s, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K_s, V_s, scale=scale)\\n271:         Z_t, _ = self.dot_attention(Q, K_t, V_t, scale=scale)\\n272:         Z_fused = self.fuse(torch.cat([Z_c, Z_s, Z_t], dim=-1))\\n273:         return self.norm(hubs + Z_fused)\\n274: \\n275: class Broadcast(nn.Module):\\n276:     \\\"\\\"\\\"Implements Section 3.2.2: Global-to-Local Affine Broadcast\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n277: \\n278:     def __init__(self, token_dim, num_c_hubs, num_s_hubs, num_t_hubs, expansion_factor=4):\\n279:         super(Broadcast, self).__init__()\\n280:         self.c_bgu = BroadcastGatedUnit(token_dim, num_c_hubs, expansion_factor)\\n281:         self.s_bgu = BroadcastGatedUnit(token_dim, num_s_hubs, expansion_factor)\\n282:         self.t_bgu = BroadcastGatedUnit(token_dim, num_t_hubs, expansion_factor)\\n283: \\n284:     def forward(self, c_orgs, s_orgs, t_orgs, c_hubs, s_hubs, t_hubs):\\n285:         c_orgs_out = self.c_bgu(c_orgs, c_hubs)\\n286:         s_orgs_out = self.s_bgu(s_orgs, s_hubs)\\n287:         t_orgs_out = self.t_bgu(t_orgs, t_hubs)\\n288:         return c_orgs_out, s_orgs_out, t_orgs_out\\n289: \\n290: \\n291: class BroadcastGatedUnit(nn.Module):\\n292:     \\\"\\\"\\\"The BGU (Broadcast Gated Unit) mechanism in the paper\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n293: \\n294:     def __init__(self, token_dim, num_hubs, expansion_factor=4):\\n295:         super(BroadcastGatedUnit, self).__init__()\\n296:         self.mlp = nn.Sequential(\\n297:             nn.Linear(num_hubs * token_dim, token_dim * expansion_factor),\\n298:             nn.ReLU(),\\n299:             nn.Linear(token_dim * expansion_factor, token_dim * 2)\\n300:         )\\n301:         self.norm = nn.LayerNorm(token_dim)\\n302: \\n303:     def forward(self, orgs, hubs):\\n304:         B = hubs.size(0)\\n305:         hubs_flat = hubs.view(B, -1)\\n306: \\n307:         hyper_params = self.mlp(hubs_flat)\\n308:         alpha, beta = hyper_params.chunk(2, dim=-1)\\n309:         alpha = alpha.unsqueeze(1)  # B x 1 x token_dim\\n310:         beta = beta.unsqueeze(1)  # B x 1 x token_dim\\n311: \\n312:         bgu_out = orgs * torch.sigmoid(alpha) + beta\\n313:         return self.norm(orgs + bgu_out)\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 255 --lines 75'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 255,\n  \"next_line\": 330,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"255: It will lead to overfitting under limited training data and add the burden of computation and storage, which may not be tolerated for an industrial online system.\\n256: Is there an elegant way to represent user’s diverse interests in one vector under limited dimension?\\n257: The local activation characteristic of user interests gives us inspiration to design a novel model named deep interest network(DIN).\\n258: Imagine when the young mother mentioned above in section 3 visits the e-commerce site, she finds the displayed new handbag cute and clicks it.\\n259: Let’s dissect the driving force of click action.\\n260: The displayed ad hits the related interests of this young mother by soft-searching her historical behaviors and finding that she had browsed similar goods of tote bag and leather handbag recently.\\n261: In other words, behaviors related to displayed ad greatly contribute to the click action.\\n262: DIN simulates this process by paying attention to the representation of locally activated interests w.r.t. given ad.\\n263: Instead of expressing all user’s diverse interests with the same vector, DIN adaptively calculate the representation vector of user interests by taking into consideration the relevance of historical behaviors w.r.t. candidate ad.\\n264: This representation vector varies over different ads.\\n265: The right part of Fig.2 illustrates the architecture of DIN.\\n266: Compared with base model, DIN introduces a novel designed local activation unit and maintains the other structures the same. Specifically, activation units are applied on the user behavior features, which performs as a weighted sum pooling to adaptively calculate user representation 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} given a candidate ad AA, as shown in Eq.(3)\\n267: (3)\\n268: 𝒗U(A)=f(𝒗A,𝒆1,𝒆2,..,𝒆H)=∑j=1Ha⁡(𝒆j,𝒗A)​𝒆j=∑j=1H𝒘j​𝒆j,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A)=f(\\\\bm{v}_{A},\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},..,\\\\bm{e}_{H})&=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}a(\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\bm{v}_{A})\\\\bm{e}_{j}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\end{split}\\n269: where {𝒆1,𝒆2,…,𝒆H}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},...,\\\\bm{e}_{H}\\\\} is the list of embedding vectors of behaviors of user UU with length of HH, 𝒗A\\\\bm{v}_{A} is the embedding vector of ad AA. In this way, 𝒗U​(A)\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A) varies over different ads. a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is a feed-forward network with output as the activation weight, as illustrated in Fig.2. Apart from the two input embedding vectors, a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) adds the out product of them to feed into the subsequent network, which is an explicit knowledge to help relevance modeling.\\n270: Local activation unit of Eq.(3) shares similar ideas with attention methods which are developed in NMT task(Bahdanau\\n271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\\\_goods\\\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n292: Take ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\\n293: Only parameters of non-zero sparse features appearing in each mini-batch needs to be updated in the scenario of SGD based optimization methods without regularization.\\n294: However, when adding ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization it needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters for each mini-batch, which leads to extremely heavy computations and is unacceptable with parameters scaling up to hundreds of millions.\\n295: Figure 3. Control function of PReLU and Dice.\\n296: In this paper, we introduce an efficient mini-batch aware regularizer, which only calculates the L2-norm over the parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch and makes the computation possible.\\n297: In fact, it is the embedding dictionary that contributes most of the parameters for CTR networks and arises the difficulty of heavy computation.\\n298: Let 𝐖∈ℝD×K\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K} denote parameters of the whole embedding dictionary, with DD as the dimensionality of the embedding vector and KK as the dimensionality of feature space.\\n299: Expand the ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization on 𝐖\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}} over samples\\n300: (4)\\n301: L2​(𝐖)=‖𝐖‖22=∑j=1K‖𝒘j‖22=∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮∑j=1KI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})&=\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\\\end{split}\\n302: where 𝒘j∈ℝD\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D} is the jj-th embedding vector,\\n303: I⁡(𝒙j≠0)I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0) denotes if the instance 𝒙\\\\bm{x} has the feature id jj, and njn_{j} denotes the number of occurrence for feature id jj in all samples. Eq.(4) can be transformed into Eq.(5) in the mini-batch aware manner\\n304: (5)\\n305: L2​(𝐖)=∑j=1K∑m=1B∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬmI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\n306: where BB denotes the number of mini-batches, ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m} denotes the mm-th mini-batch.\\n307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\\n309: Then Eq.(5) can be approximated by\\n310: (6)\\n311: L2​(𝐖)≈∑j=1K∑m=1Bαm​jnj​‖𝒘j‖22.L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})\\\\approx\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}.\\n312: In this way, we derive an approximated mini-batch aware version of ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization.\\n313: For the mm-th mini-batch, the gradient w.r.t. the embedding weights of feature jj is\\n314: (7)\\n315: 𝒘j←𝒘j−η⁡[1|ℬm|​∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬm∂L⁡(p⁡(𝒙),y)∂𝒘j+λ​αm​jnj​𝒘j],\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\leftarrow\\\\bm{w}_{j}-\\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}{\\\\frac{\\\\partial L(p(\\\\bm{x}),y)}{\\\\partial\\\\bm{w}_{j}}+\\\\lambda\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\bm{w}_{j}}\\\\right],\\n316: in which only parameters of features appearing in mm-th mini-batch participate in the computation of regularization.\\n317: 5.2. Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n318: PReLU (He\\n319: et al., 2015) is a commonly used activation function\\n320: (8)\\n321: f⁡(s)={s if ​s>0α​s if ​s≤0.=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,f(s)=\\\\begin{cases}s&\\\\text{ if }s>0\\\\\\\\\\n322: \\\\alpha s&\\\\text{ if }s\\\\leq 0.\\\\end{cases}=\\\\penalty\\\\ p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\n323: where ss is one dimension of the input of activation function f⁡(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) and p⁡(s)=I⁡(s>0)p(s)=I(s>0) is an indicator function which controls f⁡(s)f(s) to switch between two channels of f⁡(s)=sf(s)=s and f⁡(s)=α​sf(s)=\\\\alpha s. α\\\\alpha in the second channel is a learning parameter.\\n324: Here we refer to p⁡(s)p(s) as the control function. The left part of Fig.3 plots the control function of PReLU.\\n325: PReLU takes a hard rectified point with value of 00, which may be not suitable when the inputs of each layer follow different distributions.\\n326: Take this into consideration, we design a novel data adaptive activation function named Dice,\\n327: (9)\\n328: f⁡(s)=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,p⁡(s)=11+e−s−E⁡[s]V​a​r​[s]+ϵf(s)=p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\\\ \\\\ p(s)=\\\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\\\frac{s-E[s]}{\\\\sqrt{Var[s]+\\\\epsilon}}}}\\n329: with the control function to be plotted in the right part of Fig.3.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
