rank summary and direct readout through R12. The new path changes where these summaries can act, without another scorer trunk or wider tower.'},\n {'source_ref':'paper:din; archived primary text lines255-283, SHA256 83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e','verification':'read','difference':'Retain inherited target-conditioned unnormalized event sums; no exact DIN reproduction claim. The new normalized attention operates on four compressed task summaries and does not replace the event activation sums.'},\n {'source_ref':'unirank-code:INFNet; pinned primary benchmark source lines190-313; unirank-benchmark-v2 lines29-39','verification':'implementation_checked','difference':'Closest inspected external information-flow reference uses repeated categorical/sequence/task hub aggregation and affine broadcasts. Here one masked summary-to-existing-token read is inserted into a locally measured incumbent, with four compressed learned summaries, no raw sequence hub stack or affine broadcast. This is not INFNet reproduction. Its original paper paper:infnet has provenance only in the authorized archive; exact original-method overlap and priority remain unverified.'}\n],\n'inherited_components':['R12 complete source identity verified at entry against committed receipt','g0 field embeddings, masked DIN sums, relation/temporal tokens, position/content banks, factorized feedback tokens, collapsed joint memory and JointBlock','g3 private residual task scorers, shared linear rank projection and four towers via R12','all input sanitation, target-action exclusion, feedback visibility, initialization and CPU RNG behavior','frozen host data, scoring, one-pass training, optimizer split and default four-BCE loss'],\n'proposed_difference':('Add TaskSummaryExchange after feedback_memory and before JointBlock. Existing tokens query four memory slots built by a shared bias-free 112->32 projection of S_t=global_DIN+C_t+P(M_t), plus learned task identities. Two 16-wide attention heads use FP32 scores/softmax and exact masking/renormalization. Task identities enter both keys and values, so the mean-source control remains an active query-dependent learned function. A bias-free zero-initialized 32->32 output residual preserves the initial incumbent function. All slots are masked iff the authorized history is empty, and invalid query tokens are zeroed. No new tower tokens or dimensions. Candidate uses S_t privately on this route; control uses four copies of mean_t(S_t). Both retain private direct S_t in the towers. Compute the inherited task pool once, earlier in forward; it depends only on target/history, not interaction output. Append construction inside a CPU RNG fork after all inherited initialization. Estimated identical +7936 dense parameters per arm; total33077193, embeddings31793216, dense1283977, subject to host measurement.'),\n'predictions':[\n 'Under the frozen matched recipe, private exchange exceeds both mean exchange and R12 on all-four-task macro AUC; source bests are additional references, not the selection ceiling. Use the existing tie breakers for exact ties and report every task delta without adding veto thresholds.',\n 'With paired initialized states and exchange output zero, both variants reproduce R12 predictions, inherited states, Dice buffers and CPU RNG within precision tolerance. This is a required implementation gate, not a measured result.',\n 'On a nondegenerate cloned numerical fixture with open output and differing summaries, equal-mean perturbations of individual S_t leave the mean-source exchange unchanged but can change the private exchange. This diagnostic is isolated to the exchange: full direct private tower readouts can change in either model.',\n 'For empty histories the exchange update is exactly zero. On nonempty histories, learned identities keep mean-source attention query-dependent when its output is opened. Higher per-task/history-slice gains are exploratory; no slice mechanism claim is predeclared.'\n],\n'falsifiers':[\n 'Private exchange fails to exceed R12: this implementation has no demonstrated incremental utility even if it beats the mean control.',\n 'Mean exchange matches or beats private exchange: added attention/reparameterization or common history information explains the gain as well; task-specific exchange is unsupported.',\n 'Any improvement reverses across paired seeds, is smaller than observed seed variation, or does not survive justified measured cost comparison: no repeated mechanism or efficiency support.',\n 'Initial replay, mask/target-action invariance, finite FP32/BF16 backward, source-sensitivity witness or active mean-control gate fails: diagnose implementation before interpreting scores. A failed implementation is not a general mechanism refutation.'\n],\n'alternative_explanations':[\n 'New dense parameters, learned identity values or additional depth/optimization improve performance without using distinct task summaries; mean exchange is the immediate capacity/operator control.',\n 'Private versus mean changes effective information and gradient coupling, not nominal parameters alone. End-to-end training can alter embeddings, scorers and shared tokens, so the contrast does not isolate forward information from backward effects.',\n 'Additional path changes clipping norms, weight-decay effects and optimization despite identical host hyperparameters; report these where host diagnostics are available.',\n 'An early-versus-late location effect is not isolated: no equally sized after-JointBlock arm is scheduled. A positive private-minus-mean contrast supports task specificity at this chosen location, not necessity of that location.',\n 'Single-seed model selection and many prior trials cause selection optimism. Repeated same-seed R14-open is replay only. No confidence/significance claim from this screen.',\n 'Benchmarked historical delayed actions may be immature at serving time, official vocabularies/filtering use the whole window, 